ector of error terms. For the model to be identified, the rank of the matrix \\(A\\) must be equal to the number of endogenous variables. If the rank is less than the number of endogenous variables, the model is underidentified.\n\nThe implications of underidentification are significant. When a model is underidentified, it cannot yield consistent and reliable estimates of the parameters, which can lead to misleading inferences and conclusions. Researchers may find that their estimates are not unique, and different parameter values could fit the data equally well, complicating the interpretation of results.\n\nTo address underidentification, econometricians may need to impose additional restrictions or utilize instrumental variable techniques. These approaches can help to provide the necessary information to identify the model correctly. For instance, introducing valid instruments—variables that are correlated with the endogenous variables but uncorrelated with the error terms—can help achieve identification.\n\nIn summary, underidentification is a fundamental issue in econometric modeling that arises when there are insufficient equations to estimate the unknown parameters of a model. It highlights the importance of having a well-specified model with adequate restrictions to ensure that parameter estimates are unique and interpretable. Understanding and addressing underidentification is crucial for producing valid econometric analyses and drawing reliable conclusions from empirical data.\",2\r\n2248,EQUILIBRIUM PRICE,Equilibrium price is the price at which the quantity of a good demanded equals the quantity supplied.,2\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n2883,ECONOMETRICS,DEMAND AND SUPPLY MODEL,The demand and supply model is a key concept studied within econometrics,148\r\n2887,EQUILIBRIUM QUANTITY,DEMAND AND SUPPLY MODEL,Equilibrium quantity is determined by the demand and supply model,8\r\n2889,UNDERIDENTI